# class Size { constructor(w) { this.w = w } }   function make(n) { const o = {}; o.a = n; return o }
# A strict store that follows the allocation before anything else can use the object creates an own property, unless the object inherits a setter.
# So a read of that property does not answer undefined. It still does where the object was handed out before the store, where the store is sloppy, and where the class has a setter;
# and the setter is still called.
